<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/intermediate/perception">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Perception research asks <strong>how the brain builds the experienced world from sensory
    signals</strong> — and repeatedly finds construction where we assumed recording. The
    neuroscience lesson covers transduction and cortex; this one studies the constructed
    result: the world as you experience and report it. Illusions are the field's instruments —
    cases where the inference machinery is exposed, running on inputs engineered to reveal its
    rules. They matter because every "normal" perception is the same kind of act: a best guess
    that happens to be right.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="inference, ambiguity, organisation, attention — the four pillars of perception" widget="equationStep" config={percepCfg} />

  <h2>The cognitive approach: what psychology adds to the wiring</h2>
  <p>
    The cognitive approach starts from four assumptions. First, <strong>mental processes exist
    between stimulus and response</strong>: the same retinal image yields different
    perceptions, so something in between — organisation, expectation, attention — does causal
    work. Second, the mind is an <strong>information processor</strong>: signals are encoded,
    transformed, stored, and compared against stored knowledge (<em>schemas</em>), like stages
    in a pipeline that can be probed one at a time. Third, because the processing is internal,
    psychologists <strong>infer it from behaviour</strong>: reaction times, errors, and
    systematic misperceptions are the data — an illusion is a leak in the pipeline that lets us
    see one stage working. Fourth, this justifies <strong>controlled laboratory experiments</strong>:
    if the interesting machinery is inference, you engineer input to catch it in the act. Every
    study in this unit is an instance: Andrade manipulates spare attentional capacity,
    Baron-Cohen engineers a forced-choice reading of the eyes, Pozzulo stages line-ups to
    measure identification accuracy.
  </p>

  <h2>Perception as unconscious inference</h2>
  <p>
    Helmholtz's 19th-century phrase — <strong>unconscious inference</strong> — survives because
    it is exactly right: the brain treats the retinal image as ambiguous evidence and settles
    on the hypothesis that best explains it. The evidence for construction is that identical
    input yields different perceptions: the <strong>Necker cube</strong> flips between two
    3-D readings while the page sends the same 2-D pattern; in <strong>binocular
    rivalry</strong> two different images shown one per eye alternate in awareness, though
    both eyes keep reporting. In the <strong>McGurk effect</strong>, a video of a mouth saying
    "ga" dubbed with the audio "ba" yields the heard experience "da" — the brain fuses lip
    reading with sound and reports the compromise. The modern formulation is Bayesian: the
    brain combines the incoming evidence (likelihood) with prior expectations and reports the
    posterior — the most probable world. Priors explain top-down effects: the same ambiguous
    sound is heard as "lah" or "far" depending on the sentence around it.
  </p>

  <h2>Depth, size and the illusions they generate</h2>
  <p>
    The 3-D world is rebuilt from cues. <strong>Binocular</strong> cues come from having two
    eyes: <em>retinal disparity</em> (each eye gets a slightly different view; the brain's
    comparison yields stereopsis — depth from difference, exploited by random-dot stereograms,
    where depth appears from patterns with no object contours at all), and
    <em>convergence</em> (the eyes' angle for near objects). <strong>Monocular</strong> cues
    work in pictures and with one eye: occlusion, relative size, linear perspective, texture
    gradients (grain gets finer with distance), height in the plane, aerial haze, and motion
    parallax when you move. Given these, the brain maintains <strong>size constancy</strong>:
    a friend at 20 metres casts a tiny image but is perceived at full height — perceived size
    is computed as image size × computed distance. <strong>Emmert's law</strong> makes the
    arithmetic visible: an afterimage on a nearby wall looks small; the identical retinal
    signal "projected" onto a far wall looks huge — same image, computed distance ten times
    larger, perceived size follows.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classic illusions are constancy machinery misfiring on deliberately ambiguous
    drawings. The <strong>Ponzo illusion</strong> (converging railway lines make the upper
    bar look bigger — perspective says "farther", retinal size says "same", so the brain
    scales it up). The <strong>Müller-Lyer illusion</strong> (arrowheads make one line look
    longer) has been explained as a corner-and-depth prior. Crucially, it is <em>not
    universal</em>: Segall, Campbell and Herskovits (1963) found it far weaker in people from
    non-carpentered environments — where few scenes contain the rectilinear corners the
    illusion exploits. Henrich and colleagues (2010) used this as their flagship case that
    psychology's "universal" laws are often the learned statistics of WEIRD samples
    (Western, Educated, Industrialised, Rich, Democratic).
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> illusions are defects of the eye. They are properties of a
  normally functioning inference engine fed tricky inputs. Lightness is the cleanest
  demonstration: in Adelson's checker-shadow image, two squares with identical reflectance
  look black and white because the brain discounts the shadow — and no amount of being told
  changes what you see, because the computation runs below consciousness.</p>

  <h2>Gestalt organisation: how parts become objects</h2>
  <p>
    The Gestalt psychologists (Wertheimer, Köhler, Koffka) asked how scattered elements become
    unified objects, and answered with grouping principles the visual system applies without
    instruction: <strong>proximity</strong> (nearby elements group), <strong>similarity</strong>
    (alike elements group), <strong>closure</strong> (gaps are bridged — you see a circle in a
    dotted outline), <strong>good continuation</strong> (crossing lines resolve into the
    smoothest paths), <strong>common fate</strong> (things moving together group — the reason
    a flock reads as one object), and <strong>figure-ground</strong> segregation (every edge
    must be assigned to an object or its background; reversible figures like the vase/faces
    show the assignment flipping). The slogan is usually misquoted: the whole is <em>other
    than</em> the sum of the parts — grouping creates properties (a melody, a face) that exist
    at no single element. These principles are now read as a brain doing the statistically
    sensible thing: in natural scenes, similar and nearby points usually do belong to one
    object.
  </p>

  <h2>Attention: the gate between seeing and knowing</h2>
  <p>
    The eyes deliver far more than the mind processes, and <strong>selective attention</strong>
    is the gatekeeper. In dichotic-listening studies (Cherry, 1953) participants shadow one
    ear's message and report almost nothing from the unattended one — though their own name
    still breaks through, which pushed Broadbent's all-or-nothing filter model towards
    Treisman's attenuation account (unattended signal weakened, not deleted). The dramatic
    demonstrations are visual. <strong>Inattentional blindness</strong>: in Simons and
    Chabris's basketball study (1999), about half of viewers counting passes fail to see a
    person in a gorilla suit stroll through the scene. <strong>Change blindness</strong>:
    viewers miss a person swapped mid-conversation, or a wall changing colour during a blink
    — the sense of a rich, continuously updated visual world is itself a construction,
    refreshed only where attention points.
  </p>
  <p>
    Andrade's doodling study (2010) turns the gate into a practical finding. Participants
    monitored a dull, monotonous telephone message listing who would come to a party; half
    were given a shading task (doodling filled shapes) while half just listened. The doodlers
    recalled about 29% more — writing down roughly 7.5 of the 8 target names and places
    versus 5.8 for controls — and detected more of the "go to the meeting" cues. The
    interpretation is attentional, not motivational: a boring listening task under-uses
    resources, arousal drifts down, and daydreaming <em>does</em> compete for the verbal
    channel. Doodling is a motor task that absorbs spare capacity without touching the verbal
    system — a cheap external pacemaker that keeps the mind on the message. A one-line
    lesson with real reach: interference is channel-specific, and the right fidget can be
    better than forced stillness.
  </p>

  <h2>Faces, eyes and identification: perception as evidence</h2>
  <p>
    Faces get dedicated machinery: prosopagnosia (face blindness after certain brain damage)
    dissociates face recognition from object recognition, and imaging locates a
    fusiform "face area" whose activity tracks face perception. Face processing has its own
    quirks — the <strong>Thatcher illusion</strong> (a face inverted with local features
    inverted looks grotesque upright and nearly normal upside-down, revealing that upright
    faces are processed configurally, as a whole, while inverted ones are processed feature by
    feature) and the <strong>own-race bias</strong> (faces of unfamiliar groups are
    consistently harder to individuate — perceptual learning that can also be trained).
  </p>
  <p>
    Baron-Cohen and colleagues' <strong>Reading the Mind in the Eyes Test</strong> (revised
    2001) distils social perception to its minimum: 36 photographs of just the eye region,
    four mental-state words per photo, forced choice. Typical adults score around 26 of 36;
    adults with Asperger syndrome or high-functioning autism average around 22; women score
    slightly higher on average than men. The test operationalises a claim with a strong
    perceptual component — that mental states can be read from subtle eye-region cues
    (gaze direction, lid tension) — and companion imaging work ties accurate reading to
    activity in the superior temporal gyrus and amygdala. It is a perception study that
    doubles as a clinical instrument.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pozzulo and Lindsay's line-up research (1998) closes the loop on consequences.
    Presenting children (about 10-12) and adults with target-present and target-absent
    line-ups showed a systematic pattern: children are especially prone to
    <strong>false identification when the culprit is absent</strong> — asked "is this the
    person?", the pressure to choose wins, and a plausible face gets picked. Adult accuracy
    is higher, and later work found an <strong>own-age bias</strong> (people identify faces
    of their own age group more accurately). The courtroom implication is direct:
    identification evidence is a perceptual measurement, subject to attention, prior
    exposure and grouping biases like any other — which is why procedure design (double-blind
    line-ups, explicit "the person may not be here" instructions) is now treated as part of
    the measurement instrument.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Colour: the paradigm construction.</strong> The retina records wavelength
    ratios that shift enormously with illumination — an apple reflects very different
    spectra indoors and outdoors, yet stays red. <strong>Colour constancy</strong> works
    by discounting the illuminant: estimate the light source, correct the surface. The
    machinery is visible when it breaks, and in 2015 it broke in public: a photograph of
    a dress split viewers almost exactly into "blue-and-black" and "white-and-gold"
    camps, because the image's ambiguous illumination let different priors (daylight
    versus artificial light, held with different strengths) drive opposite constancy
    solutions. One image, two constructed worlds — and neither camp was wrong about its
    own perception, which is the entire lesson of this page compressed into a JPEG.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Two streams, two constructions.</strong> The classic neuropsychology adds
    architecture to the story: visual processing splits into a ventral "what" stream
    (identification, conscious perception) and a dorsal "where/how" stream (guiding
    action). Goodale and Milner's patient D.F., with ventral damage, could not name or
    match the orientation of a slot — yet posted a letter into it accurately. Perception
    and the perception-for-action system construct the world twice, by different routes —
    and usually agree, which is why we never notice the division until the clinic
    reveals it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Predictive processing.</strong> The Bayesian story has a modern, radical form
    (Friston, Clark): the cortex continuously predicts its input and perception is the
    prediction, corrected only by prediction errors. On this view a hallucination is a prior
    running unopposed (it fires in psychosis and in sensory deprivation, where the brain
    "fills in" with expected content), and an illusion is a correct prior misapplied. The
    framework explains why perception is so stable despite saccades — during each eye jump,
    the visual signal is suppressed and the world is largely <em>assumed</em> to continue.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Perceptual learning is real and specific.</strong> Radiologists see pathology in
    scans novices cannot locate even when told where it is; chicken sexers, wine tasters and
    chess masters show the same channel-specific plasticity. The learning is fast enough to
    matter and narrow enough to need explanation: it changes the evidence-extraction stage,
    not just decision criteria — which is why expertise fails to transfer wholesale.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How much of grouping is innate versus tuned by the
    statistics of a species' visual world (the carpentered-world result cuts both ways)?
    Where does perception end and cognition begin — can desire, language or belief
    penetrate early vision, or only attention and interpretation (the "cognitive
    impenetrability" debate)? And can the eyes-test framework measure anything beyond
    a test-specific skill? Perception remains the liveliest meeting point of philosophy,
    neuroscience and laboratory psychology — because every answer is checked against the one
    instrument that never lies about the question: what people actually experience.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Goldstein, Sensation and Perception; Helmholtz on unconscious inference; Segall, Campbell & Herskovits (1963); Simons & Chabris (1999); Andrade (2010), Applied Cognitive Psychology; Baron-Cohen et al. (2001), the revised Eyes Test; Pozzulo & Lindsay (1998); Henrich, Heine & Norenzayan (2010); Clark, Surfing Uncertainty.</p>

